\section{Problems}

\subsection*{Problem 1 \hfill \normalfont\small [Notation] -- Easy}
\texttt{EUR/USD} is quoted at 1.0875. Under the convention S =\allowbreak{} domestic currency per unit of foreign currency, identify the domestic and foreign currencies. If \texttt{EUR/USD} rises to 1.0950, who benefits: a trader long \texttt{EUR/USD} or short \texttt{EUR/USD}?
\vspace{2.5mm}

\subsection*{Problem 2 \hfill \normalfont\small [Notation] -- Easy}
\texttt{USD/JPY} moves from 149.20 to 149.68. How many pips is the move? If the platform shows 149.683, what is the final decimal digit commonly called?
\vspace{2.5mm}

\subsection*{Problem 3 \hfill \normalfont\small [Notation] -- Easy}
A corporate treasurer expects to receive EUR 5 million in three months and reports in USD. Explain the FX exposure and the sign of the hedge using \texttt{EUR/USD} forwards.
\vspace{2.5mm}

\subsection*{Problem 4 \hfill \normalfont\small [Arbitrage] -- Medium}
Suppose \texttt{EUR/USD} =\allowbreak{} 1.1000, \texttt{USD/JPY} =\allowbreak{} 150.00, while \texttt{EUR/JPY} is quoted at 166.50. Is there triangular arbitrage? Construct one cycle starting with JPY 150,000,000 and compute the approximate profit ignoring bid-ask spreads.
\vspace{2.5mm}

\subsection*{Problem 5 \hfill \normalfont\small [Quote Inversion] -- Medium}
If \texttt{EUR/USD} =\allowbreak{} 1.2500, compute USD/EUR. If \texttt{EUR/USD} increases by 1\%, approximately what percentage change occurs in USD/EUR? Explain why the sign flips.
\vspace{2.5mm}

\subsection*{Problem 6 \hfill \normalfont\small [Forwards] -- Medium}
Derive continuous-compounding covered interest parity (CIP): F(0,T) =\allowbreak{} S0 exp((r\_d-r\_f)T), using a no-arbitrage cash-and-carry argument.
\vspace{2.5mm}

\subsection*{Problem 7 \hfill \normalfont\small [Forwards] -- Medium}
\texttt{EUR/USD} spot is 1.0800, USD rate 4.5\%, EUR rate 2.5\%, T =\allowbreak{} 0.5 years, continuously compounded. Compute the 6M forward outright and forward points.
\vspace{2.5mm}

\subsection*{Problem 8 \hfill \normalfont\small [Forwards] -- Medium}
Under annual simple compounding, derive the forward formula F =\allowbreak{} S(1+r\_d T)/(1+r\_f T). Using S=\allowbreak{}1.3000, r\_d=\allowbreak{}5\%, r\_f=\allowbreak{}3\%, T=\allowbreak{}0.25, compute F.
\vspace{2.5mm}

\subsection*{Problem 9 \hfill \normalfont\small [Forwards] -- Medium}
A dealer quotes 3M \texttt{EUR/USD} forward points +28 and 6M points +61, where one pip is 0.0001. Estimate 4M points by linear interpolation in time and state one limitation of this approach.
\vspace{2.5mm}

\subsection*{Problem 10 \hfill \normalfont\small [FX Swap] -- Medium}
Spot \texttt{EUR/USD} is 1.0